Chapter 3 — Part 1
All Might lifted a hand at the station turnoff. The muscle form caught the streetlight, smile easy.
“Get some rest, young Beardy. Tomorrow’s still a school day whether the crater agrees or not.”
Curtis nodded once. “You too.”
They split. All Might headed toward the patrol route that would take him back into the city proper. Curtis turned the other way, duffel over one shoulder, and walked into the dark.
The campus lights fell behind him. He didn’t go to the dorms. The transfer paperwork had a room listed, but the building still smelled like fresh paint and unfinished schedules, and he wasn’t ready to plant himself somewhere that expected him to stay put. He had money. Not a fortune, but enough from odd work back home and the quiet stipend Nezu had arranged. What he didn’t have yet was a rhythm. A place that stayed open late. A reason to be somewhere when the uniform came off.
He found a laundromat six blocks off the main student paths. Fluorescent lights. Two other people—one asleep in a plastic chair, one folding shirts with headphones in. Curtis fed coins into the machine, stripped the dirt-streaked U.A. jacket and pants into the drum, and sat on the low bench while the cycle ran. The blood at his hairline had dried. His ribs ached in the clean way that meant nothing was torn. He watched the uniform tumble and didn’t check his phone.
When the dryer finished he changed in the narrow bathroom, civilian clothes from the duffel, braids redone tight. The uniform went into a plastic bag at the bottom of the pack. He stepped back out into the night air. Cooler now. The low, passive warmth in his chest stayed exactly where it had been all day—quiet, constant, unnamed.
He started walking.
Job first. Something with late hours or early ones, the kind that didn’t care about a hero course schedule as long as the work got done. Then a net café if the job didn’t come with a back room. He had the cash for a few nights. Stability was the part he still needed to build. The city moved around him—trains in the distance, a convenience store bell, someone laughing too loud outside a bar. Curtis kept his eyes on the street ahead and the handwritten signs in the windows, looking for the ones that said help wanted and meant it.
The uniform was clean. The crater was behind him. The rest of the night was still open.
They walked. The girl kept the flyer stack hugged to her chest like it might steady her. Every time the sidewalk narrowed she drifted closer instead of giving him room. Her shoulder brushed his arm once. She didn’t apologize. Color stayed high on her cheeks.
“I’m Hina,” she said after half a block. Voice softer than the pitch she’d started with. “The club’s called Lumen. Most of the hosts are already booked solid for opening week. You’d… stand out.”
Curtis glanced at the street signs. “Curtis. And I ain’t looking to stand out. Looking for hours.”
Hina laughed, small and nervous. “Those aren’t mutually exclusive there. The customers pay more when they like looking at who they’re talking to.” She risked another glance up at him. The Free Love pull sat warm and constant between them; it made her bold in inches. “You have an accent. American?”
“Appalachia. Mountains.” He shifted the duffel. “You always hand these out this late?”
“Only when they need the extra bodies.” Her fingers tightened on the stack. “Most people take the flyer and keep walking. You actually asked about work.” She hesitated, then added, quieter, “It’s nice. When someone listens instead of just staring at the outfit.”
Curtis looked at her then—really looked, the way he checked trail markers. Short jacket, glossy hair, the blush that hadn’t faded. He didn’t connect it to anything in his chest. “Outfit’s doing its job. You’re the one talking, though. Makes it easier to know if the place is worth the stop.”
Hina’s breath caught. She had to look forward again so she wouldn’t trip. “It’s worth it. Even if you only stay long enough to ask the manager. She’s fair. And if they don’t have a spot that fits your schedule…” She trailed off, then tried again, lighter. “There’s a net café two streets over. Open all night. Clean booths. I go there sometimes after shifts when I don’t want the train.”
“Good to know.” Curtis’s tone stayed even. Practical. “You heading there after this?”
“Maybe.” The word came out softer than she meant. She tucked a strand of hair behind her ear and didn’t quite manage to hide the smile. “Depends if you still need directions once we’re done at Lumen. I don’t mind walking you. It’s on my way.”
They turned the corner. Neon from the club’s sign already reached the end of the block—pink and white, promising low lights and expensive drinks. Hina slowed without deciding to. Curtis matched her pace. The low, unnamed warmth in his chest stayed exactly where it always was, quiet and constant, while she kept finding reasons to glance at him and he kept answering like the night was only about hours and train lines.
“Almost there,” she said.
“Lead on.”
She did. A little closer than before.
The neon got brighter as they closed the last block. Lumen’s sign hummed pink against the dark. Hina’s steps had slowed again. She kept the flyer stack tight to her ribs and stole another look at him before she spoke.
“You really don’t know what a host club is,” she said. Not teasing. Just confirming. “Even after the flyer.”
“Talking. Drinking. People paying for the time.” Curtis shrugged the duffel higher. “That’s what you said. Rest of it I can learn if they hire me. Or I walk if they don’t.”
Hina’s mouth curved. The blush had settled into something steadier. “Most guys our age already know. Or they pretend they do. You’re just… asking.” She hesitated, then added, “It’s not only talking. Some of the hosts flirt. That’s part of the job. The customers like feeling wanted. You’d be good at the looking part. The rest you’d have to decide if you want.”
Curtis considered that while they waited for the light. “Flirting for pay. Ain’t the same as meaning it.”
“No,” she agreed, quiet. “It’s not.” Her shoulder brushed his again when they stepped off the curb. She didn’t move away. “I only hand out flyers. I’m not one of the girls inside. But I hear things. The ones who last are the ones who can make somebody feel like the hour belongs to them. Even if it doesn’t.”
“Sounds like work.” His accent stayed even. “Same as any other. You show up, you do the job, you leave when the shift’s done.”
Hina laughed under her breath. “You make it sound simple.”
“Most things are till somebody complicates them.” He glanced at her. “You like it? Handing these out. Walking people over.”
She was quiet long enough that the crosswalk signal changed again. “I like the nights when somebody actually stops,” she said finally. “Most don’t. Or they take the flyer and make a joke. You asked about hours. And you didn’t stare at my legs while you did it.” The admission came out softer. She looked straight ahead. “It’s… nice. Being listened to.”
Curtis nodded like that tracked. “You talk clear. Easier to listen.”
They reached the club doors. Bass leaked through the frame. Hina stopped with her hand on the handle but didn’t pull it yet. She turned enough to look up at him, flyers still clutched tight, the Free Love warmth sitting low and patient between them.
“If they say yes,” she said, “and you take a shift… I could show you the net café after. So you know where it is. In case the back room isn’t an option.” Her voice stayed careful. “Only if you want. I don’t mind the walk.”
Curtis met her eyes. The low pull in his chest didn’t flare. He still didn’t have a name for it. “If the timing works. Yeah.”
Hina’s smile reached her eyes this time. She pulled the door open. Warm air and low music spilled out.
“Then let’s go ask.”
Hina’s fingers tightened on the door handle until the metal bit.
