The manager’s office sat behind a beaded curtain and a second, heavier door. Janice looked up from a tablet the second they stepped in. Late twenties, maybe, or older and wearing it well—sharp bob, tailored black dress, a thin silver ring on her right hand. Her eyes tracked Curtis from boots to braids in one pass and stayed there. The professional smile she started with warmed and stuck.
“Hina. You brought someone useful.” She set the tablet down. No handshake offered yet. Just the look. “Name?”
“Curtis Beardy.” He stayed by the door, duffel still on his shoulder. “Looking for night hours. Classes during the day. I can start whenever the schedule allows.”
Janice didn’t ask for a résumé. She didn’t ask for ID beyond a glance at the U.A. student card he offered when she nodded at his pocket. Free Love sat in the room the way it always did—low, passive, constant—and it pulled her attention tighter the longer he stood there. Her thighs shifted under the desk. She crossed her legs slower than she needed to and didn’t look away from his mouth when he finished talking.
“U.A. hero course,” she said. “Exchange. You’ll draw eyes whether you try or not. That’s useful here.” She tapped the tablet once, then raised her voice toward the side door. “Tatsuya. In here.”
The door opened. The man who stepped through was young—twenty-three, twenty-five at most—tall, sharp-jawed, dressed in the club’s black-on-black with the collar open. A matching silver ring sat on his left hand. He took Curtis in with a quick, practiced once-over, then looked at Janice. Something unspoken moved between them. She smiled, small and pleased.
“This is Tatsuya,” she said. “Senior host. He’ll show you the floor, the timing, what the customers actually pay for. You listen to him, you keep the hours I give you, and you don’t make problems with the girls or the regulars. Do that and the job’s yours. Trial shift starts in twenty minutes if your bag can live behind the bar.”
Tatsuya inclined his head. Polite. Curious. “Welcome. We’ll keep it simple tonight. Watch first. Talk when I tell you to. Most of it is reading the table.”
Curtis nodded once. Practical. “Understood.”
Janice’s gaze lingered on him another second—warm, already calculating the week’s bookings—then flicked to Hina. “You can clock the flyer run. Or stay and watch the trial if you want. Your choice.”
Hina’s cheeks were still flushed. She hugged the remaining flyers and didn’t quite manage to sound casual. “I’ll stay. For a bit.”
Janice’s smile widened just slightly. She already knew. “Then both of you follow Tatsuya. Curtis—leave the duffel. We’ll find you a locker that locks.”
Curtis set the bag down. The low, unnamed warmth in his chest stayed exactly where it had been all night. He still didn’t have a name for it. He followed Tatsuya toward the floor while Janice watched him go, ring glinting, already rewriting the evening’s schedule in her head.
Tatsuya pushed through the beaded curtain and onto the floor. Low amber light. Velvet booths. The bass sat under everything instead of on top of it. A few early tables were already occupied—businessmen, a pair of women in sharp dresses, one regular who lifted his glass when he saw Tatsuya and got a nod back.
“Rules first,” Tatsuya said, voice pitched so it stayed between them. He walked Curtis along the edge of the room instead of through the middle. “You don’t sit unless you’re invited or I put you there. You don’t push drinks. You don’t touch unless they ask, and even then you keep it above the table until you know the customer. Janice handles the money. You handle the hour they paid for. Make them feel like it was theirs.”
Curtis tracked the layout while he listened. Exits. Bar. The narrow hall that led to the private rooms. Two hostesses worked a center table—short dresses, practiced smiles. One of them glanced over when Tatsuya passed with the new height and the braids. Her smile flickered, real for a second, before she caught it and turned back to her customer. The other looked longer. Color touched her cheeks. She shifted in her seat.
Tatsuya noticed. He didn’t comment. “You’ll get stared at. Use it or ignore it. Both work. Just don’t freeze.”
“Not planning to.”
They stopped at the bar. The bartender—a woman with silver rings and a low neckline—set two waters down without being asked. Her eyes stayed on Curtis a beat past professional. She wiped the same spot on the counter twice.
“Trial’s simple,” Tatsuya continued. “You stand with me for the first table. Watch how I open. Second table, you take the greeting. I’ll cut in if you miss the read. After that we see if Janice wants you on a short sit with one of the regulars who likes new faces.” He glanced at Curtis. “You don’t have to flirt like the posters. Some of them pay more for quiet. Just don’t make them feel ignored.”
Curtis nodded. The low warmth in his chest stayed even while the bartender found another reason to linger and one of the hostesses at the center table laughed a little too brightly at something that wasn’t that funny. He filed it the same way he always did. New room. People looking. Nothing that changed the job.
Hina had followed them as far as the curtain. She stood just inside it now, flyers still in her arms, watching. Her cheeks were flushed again. She didn’t come closer.
Tatsuya checked his watch. “First table’s mine in four minutes. Stay half a step back. Answer if they ask you something. Otherwise let me lead.”
“Understood.”
“Good.” Tatsuya picked up his water. “Then let’s go make them glad they came in tonight.”
He started toward the booth. Curtis followed, easy and quiet, while the floor settled around the new presence and more than one pair of eyes tracked the tall exchange student who still thought the only thing happening was a trial shift.
The booth held three—two men in loosened ties and a woman in a silk blouse who had already claimed the corner seat. They looked up when Tatsuya stopped at the edge of the table. Their attention snagged on Curtis and stayed.
“Evening,” Tatsuya said, easy. “Brought someone new to watch the floor. Curtis. First night.”
The woman smiled, slow. One of the men leaned forward, elbow on the table. “He’s tall. Quiet type?” His eyes ran the measurement—shoulders, braids, the way Curtis stood half a step back like he’d been told. “Does he do the upstairs rooms? The expensive ones. Full VIP. We’d pay the premium if he’s taking appointments already.”
Tatsuya’s smile didn’t move. He shook his head once, clean. “Not tonight. First shift. We don’t cross those lines on day one, and we don’t offer what an employee hasn’t agreed to. He watches. He greets if you want. That’s the menu.”
The second man exhaled, disappointed but not pushing. The woman kept looking at Curtis, thumb tracing the rim of her glass. “Shame. He’d do well up there.” She didn’t say the rest. She didn’t have to.
Curtis heard the words and didn’t catch the weight. Upstairs. Expensive. Premium. He filed them with the rest of the club’s layout—private rooms, higher tabs, things he could learn if the job stuck. He stayed quiet, hands loose at his sides, and let Tatsuya handle the table the way he’d been told.
Tatsuya kept the hour light after that. Drinks. Small talk. A story about the grand-opening week that made the woman laugh. Curtis answered once when she asked where he was from—“Appalachia”—and watched the way her eyes lingered on his mouth afterward. He didn’t think about why. New face. Trial shift. People stared.
When the table’s time wound down, Tatsuya stepped back and nodded Curtis with him toward the bar. The low lights followed them. Behind them, the woman was already asking the floor manager about later bookings.
Tatsuya waited until they were clear of the booth. His voice stayed low. “They asked if you do the private VIP services. The ones that cost extra and happen upstairs.” He met Curtis’s eyes. “I told them no. First night. We don’t put anyone in those rooms until they’ve been here long enough to know exactly what they’re agreeing to, and until they’ve said yes themselves. Janice is strict about it. So am I.”
Curtis nodded, still turning the words over. “Upstairs is different work.”
“It can be.” Tatsuya’s expression didn’t change. “I’ll explain the full menu later. After the shift. Not in the middle of the floor. For now, you did fine. Next table’s yours to open if you want it.”
