They kept walking. Curtis still didn’t know the exact thing that had been asked. He only knew Tatsuya had shut it down clean, and that the job—so far—had rules he could follow. The low, unnamed warmth in his chest stayed quiet and constant while the club moved around them and more than one customer watched the tall new host who hadn’t said yes to anything yet.
The floor had thinned to closing lights and the low hum of the bar fridges. Curtis sat at the far end of the counter with a glass of water, uniform shirt sleeves rolled once. Tatsuya took the stool beside him. Hina hovered a step back, flyer bag empty over her shoulder, cheeks still carrying the last of the night’s color.
